syntax:
To declare a virtual function, precede its declaration in the base class with the keyword `virtual`.  Derived classes can then override the function by providing their own implementation with the same signature.

```cpp
class Base {
public:
  virtual void myFunction() { std::cout << "Base class function\n"; }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
  void myFunction() override { std::cout << "Derived class function\n"; }
};
```

**Important Notes:**
* Using `override` (C++11 and later) is a best practice as it helps catch errors where the derived class doesn't correctly implement the virtual function.
* If a function is not declared `virtual` in the base class, it will be called based on the static type (compile-time binding), not the dynamic type (runtime binding).
*  Pure virtual functions (`virtual void myFunction() = 0;`) make a class abstract; you can't create objects of an abstract class, only derived classes that provide concrete implementations can be instantiated.
